<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eustace II, Count of Boulogne</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1015–1087</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eustace II was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boulogne-sur-mer">Boulogne</a> from about 1049 and one of the leading allies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hastings">Battle of Hastings</a> in 1066, where he appears on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry</a>. He received large estates in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His sons included Eustace III, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/godfrey-of-bouillon">Godfrey of Bouillon</a>, the first ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, and Baldwin I, its first king.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eustace-ii-count-of-boulogne.jpg" alt="Eustace II, Count of Boulogne" width="320" height="454" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Myrabella · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bayeux_Tapestry_scene55_Eustach.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
